### to-spec/INITIATIVE-FORMAT.md [missing]
--- M/to-spec/INITIATIVE-FORMAT.md
+++ A/to-spec/INITIATIVE-FORMAT.md
@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+# Initiative format
+
+Project-local, divergent by design. How an initiative is named and where its entry lives are
+[the development process](../../docs/process.md#naming)'s. This shelf holds what the document argues and
+in what order.
+
+An initiative exists when several requests only mean anything read together — when shaping each against
+its neighbours' edges would produce a worse whole than shaping all of them against the journey. One
+customer asking for one thing is a spec. A pipeline that three customers each asked one piece of is an
+initiative.
+
+An initiative argues; it never specifies. Every behavior it touches belongs to a ticket under it, and the
+initiative's job is to make those tickets answerable to the whole. A sentence here that could be an
+acceptance criterion belongs in a ticket instead.
+
+## The skeleton
+
+```md
+# {The journey, in the words the customers used}
+- **Notion:** {url}
+- **Status:** {where the align stands, and what is not committed until it settles}
+
+{Lead: the journey end to end, then why it is one thing.}
+
+## Why this exists as one thing
+## The players
+## The real-world workflow today
+## How the product touches this today
+## Members
+## Open questions — the align agenda
+## Boundaries
+```
+
+`Status` is the align's state, not delivery's — the tickets carry that on the board. It says plainly what
+downstream work is held: *"Draft — journey align in progress. No UI shapes are committed until this
+settles."*
+
+| Section | Rule |
+|---|---|
+| Lead | The journey end to end in one paragraph — who acts, what they record, who watches, how it is paid for. Then the sentence that earns the document: these arrived separately, and are argued as one so the tickets are shaped by the whole. |
+| `Why this exists as one thing` | The evidence, named: which customer asked for which piece and when. State the relationship the product only half-holds today. Without this a reader cannot tell the journey from a wish. |
+| `The players` | One bullet per actor, saying what it does and what it pays or is paid. Each splits into _Known:_ — sourced from a request or already built — and _To confirm in the align:_. A need is never invented here. |
+| `The real-world workflow today` | Numbered steps of what the people actually do, product-agnostic. Each step that has one carries a _Gap:_ naming what is not yet known. An actor who joins the workflow only partway says so after the list. |
+| `How the product touches this today` | The same steps mapped onto what the product already expresses, ending each with what is absent. `nothing` is the most useful entry in this section. |
+| `Members` | The tickets under this initiative, grouped by stage of the journey rather than by status, each marked `active` or `done`. Closed tickets stay listed so the rollup reads whole. Surfaces and concerns it stands on are listed as referenced, not owned. |
+| `Open questions — the align agenda` | Numbered, in the order they must be answered, because each may reshape the members. A resolved one keeps its number and is answered inline as _Resolved:_, with any remaining part left explicit as _Open:_. |
+| `Boundaries` | What a reader will expect here and won't find, each with the journey that owns it instead. Name what this initiative deliberately does not build even though a player needs it. |
+
+## Conventions
+
+- A member ticket names this initiative in its `## Parent`; the initiative does not restate what the
+  ticket says.
+- Cite edge records, concerns and tickets by relative link; never restate them.
+- Answer a question inline in bold or as _Resolved:_, keeping the question readable.
+- Wrap at ~100 columns.

